  [3mCosts:[0m persistence becomes caller discipline. Today there's one caller so the risk is theoretical, but you've said
  more forms are coming, and if any of them logs in, that one is now responsible for remembering.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mC. A [38;5;153mSession.login()[39m facade.[0m
  [38;5;153msession.js[39m owns the auth flow: [38;5;153mSession.login(username, password)[39m calls the raw login and persists the result. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  calls the facade.
  [3mBuys:[0m one auth surface for all future forms, and the raw login stays pure behind it.
  [3mCosts:[0m a layer of indirection introduced for a function that is still a stub, with only one consumer. This is the
  option to revisit if a second login entry point actually appears.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m You said later forms need to [3mread[0m the userId, which means there's one writer and several readers —
  so the thing worth protecting is that the write always happens, not that [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays pure. A enforces that
  structurally. B's purity advantage is real but buys less here, and C builds a facade for a second consumer that
  doesn't exist yet.

  Two things I'd hold constant across all three, since they're consequences rather than preferences:
  - [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m throws in some privacy modes and when a page is opened from [38;5;153mfile://[39m in certain browsers. The store
    wraps its access in try/catch and degrades to in-memory rather than breaking login.
  - [38;5;153mlogin()[39m will become async when it really calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. I'll shape the return so that change doesn't ripple
    into callers.
